       In early filesystems, the value returned by tteellllddiirr() was a simple file
       offset within a directory.  Modern filesystems use tree or hash
       structures, rather than flat tables, to represent directories.  On such
       filesystems, the value returned by tteellllddiirr() (and used internally by
       rreeaaddddiirr(3)) is a "cookie" that is used by the implementation to derive
       a position within a directory.  Application programs should treat this
